26904296875, -0.0298614501953125, 0.01345062255859375, -0.0244598388671875, -0.016204833984375, 0.04058837890625, 0.01861572265625, 0.0007872581481933594, 0.00936126708984375, 0.002071380615234375, 0.0253753662109375, 0.0188751220703125, -0.0265045166015625, -0.0214385986328125, 0.007022857666015625, 0.01380157470703125, 0.003253936767578125, -0.01959228515625, -0.01132965087890625, -0.026947021484375, -0.00836944580078125, 0.0134429931640625, 0.0020008087158203125, -0.00563812255859375, 0.0116424560546875, -0.015777587890625, 0.007572174072265625, -0.008758544921875, 0.0034961700439453125, -0.0138702392578125, 0.033538818359375, 0.030609130859375, 0.0295867919921875, 0.001750946044921875, 0.0161590576171875, 0.0426025390625, -0.01258087158203125, 0.0212554931640625, -0.02862548828125, 0.0279693603515625, 0.0151519775390625, -0.0408935546875, 0.0143280029296875, -0.021392822265625, 0.0095977783203125, 0.028350830078125, -0.02789306640625, -0.00232696533203125, 0.00373077392578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Related Work**\n\nDistrict heating networks are critical for decarbonizing urban heat supply, but their efficient operation is challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, the need for thermal storage, and increasingly complex, meshed network topologies. Existing optimization approaches\u2014including mixed-integer linear programming [1], convex relaxations [2], and heuristic methods [3]\u2014struggle with the nonlinearities and combinatorial complexity inherent in these systems, especially for meshed networks, multiple time steps, and realistic operational constraints. Most prior work is limited to tree-like networks, linearized models, or small-scale instances, and does not address the full nonlinear, meshed, multi-supplier, and storage-coupled setting.\n\n**Novelty and Comparison with Prior Work**\n\nOur work introduces the first global optimization framework for stationary nonlinear district heating networks with general (meshed) topologies, multiple suppliers, and thermal storage, capturing nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling. In contrast to [1], which is restricted to linearized or tree-like networks, and [2], which applies convex relaxations that do not scale to meshed topologies, our approach directly handles nonlinearities and cycles. Unlike [3], which uses heuristics without optimality guarantees, we provide a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm with explicit cycle elimination, symmetry exploitation, and constraint reduction. Table 1 summarizes the key differences:\n\n| Feature                        | [1] MILP | [2] Convex Relaxation | [3] Heuristic | This Work |\n|--------------------------------|----------|----------------------|---------------|-----------|\n| Meshed networks                | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Nonlinear heat loss            | \u2717        | \u2713 (approximate)      | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Multiple suppliers/storage     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Global optimality guarantees   | \u2713        | \u2713 (relaxed)          | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Explicit cycle elimination     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Symmetry exploitation          | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Constraint reduction           | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Primal heuristics              | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n\n**2. Precise Problem Statement**\n\nWe consider the following stationary optimization problem for a district heating network over a single time step.\n\n**Sets:**\n- \\( V \\): set of nodes (junctions, consumers, suppliers, storage units)\n- \\( E \\subseteq V \\times V \\): set of directed edges (pipes)\n- \\( S \\subseteq V \\): set of supplier nodes\n- \\( D \\subseteq V \\): set of demand (consumer) nodes\n- \\( M \\subseteq V \\): set of mixing nodes\n- \\( \\mathcal{C} \\): set of fundamental cycles in \\( G \\)\n\n**Parameters:**\n- \\( d_i \\): net demand at node \\( i \\) (\\( >0 \\) for consumers, \\( <0 \\) for suppliers)\n- \\( C_i(x_i) \\): cost function for supplier \\( i \\)\n- \\( \\alpha_{ik} \\): heat loss coefficient for pipe \\( (i,k) \\)\n- \\( L_{ik} \\): length of pipe \\( (i,k) \\)\n- \\( c_p \\): specific heat capacity of water\n- \\( T_{amb} \\): ambient temperature\n- \\( T_{\\min}, T_{\\max} \\): minimum and maximum allowable temperatures\n\n**Variables:**\n- \\( f_{ik} \\geq 0 \\): mass flow on edge \\( (i,k) \\)\n- \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\): inlet and outlet temperatures on edge \\( (i,k) \\)\n- \\( x_i \\): heat supplied by supplier \\( i \\)\n- \\( Q_{ik} \\): heat delivered by pipe \\( (i,k) \\)\n- \\( s_j \\): storage state at storage node \\( j \\) (if applicable)\n\n**Optimization Problem:**\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\min_{f, T, x} \\quad & \\sum_{i \\in S} C_i(x_i) \\\\\n\\text{s.t.} \\quad\n& \\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} - \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} f_{ik} = d_i, \\quad \\forall i \\in V \\tag{1} \\\\\n& T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} = T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - \\alpha_{ik}(T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{amb}) L_{ik}, \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{2} \\\\\n& Q_{ik} = f_{ik} c_p (T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{ik}^{\\text{out}}), \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{3} \\\\\n& T_i^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} T_{ji}^{\\text{out}}}{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji}}, \\quad \\forall i \\in M \\tag{4} \\\\\n& f_{ik} \\geq 0, \\quad T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\in [T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}], \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{5} \\\\\n& x_i = \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} Q_{ik}, \\quad \\forall i \\in S \\tag{6} \\\\\n& s_j^{\\text{out}} = s_j^{\\text{in}} + \\sum_{k: (j,k) \\in E} Q_{jk} - \\sum_{i: (i,j) \\in E} Q_{ij}, \\quad \\forall j \\in \\text{storage nodes} \\tag{7} \\\\\n& \\sum_{(i,k) \\in C} f_{ik} = 0, \\quad \\forall C \\in \\mathcal{C} \\text{ with } \\sum_{i \\in C} d_i = 0 \\tag{8} \\\\\n& \\text{(Symmetry, redundancy, and other constraints as detailed below)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n**3. Justification of Model Simplifications**\n\nThe stationary model neglects hydraulic and transient dynamics, assumes perfect mixing at junctions, and omits pressure constraints. These simplifications are justified for operational planning horizons (15\u201360 minutes), where hydraulic transients settle within seconds to a few minutes [4], and temperature dynamics are slow relative to control intervals. Empirical studies [2,5] show that, for typical district heating networks with pipe lengths <5 km and demand variations <10% per hour, steady-state models yield dispatch solutions within 2\u20135% of full dynamic simulations. Pressure drops are managed by separate hydraulic controllers [6], and their impact on thermal dispatch is negligible unless the network operates near hydraulic limits or under rapid demand fluctuations. For networks with long pipes (>10 km), highly variable demand, or pressure-sensitive operation, dynamic and hydraulic effects become significant and the stationary model may yield suboptimal or infeasible solutions.\n\n**4. Methodological Enhancements**\n\nWe improve the computational performance of spatial branch-and-bound for the above problem via the following algorithmic innovations:\n\n**A. Cycle Elimination Constraints**\n\nTo prevent physically infeasible cyclic flows, we enumerate a fundamental cycle basis \\( \\mathcal{C} \\) (using Paton's algorithm, \\( O(|E|+|V|) \\)), and for each cycle \\( C \\) with zero net supply/demand, enforce:\n\\[\n\\sum_{(i,k) \\in C^+} f_{ik} - \\sum_{(k,i) \\in C^-} f_{ki} = 0\n\\]\nwhere \\( C^+ \\) and \\( C^- \\) are the forward and reverse edges in the cycle orientation. For cycles with net supply/demand, we adjust the constraint accordingly. This reduces the feasible set to physically meaningful flows and strengthens relaxations. The number of constraints is \\( |E| - |V| + 1 \\).\n\n**B. Symmetry Exploitation**\n\nWe detect isomorphic subgraphs in the supply and return networks using the VF2 algorithm [7] (\\( O(n^2) \\) for practical graphs). For each symmetry class, we solve only one representative subproblem and map solutions to equivalent nodes, reducing redundant computation. For example, if two branches from a mixing node are structurally identical and have identical parameters, only one needs to be explored in the branch-and-bound tree. This reduces the effective search space by a factor equal to the size of the symmetry class.\n\n**C. Constraint Reduction**\n\nAt each mixing node with \\( k \\) inlets, only \\( k-1 \\) of the mixing constraints (Eq. 4) are linearly independent. We construct a spanning tree over the mixing subgraph and retain only constraints corresponding to tree edges, removing cycles. This reduces the number of equality constraints from \\( |E| \\) to \\( |V|-1 \\) in the mixing subgraph, improving numerical conditioning and solver efficiency.\n\n**D. Primal Heuristics**\n\nWe implement a greedy heuristic: starting from suppliers, assign flows to meet"}}